WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.080
Forest bathing is a term
coined in Japan in the 1980s.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.720
Since then, we've learned it has
benefits beyond just the exercise.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.880
In this video, we'll look at
some of those benefits,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.080
and whether or not you
actually need to visit a forest

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.601
to experience them.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:25.253
"Boosting Anticancer Immunity and
Natural Killer Cell Function with Forest Bathing"

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.960
Previously, I showed how exposure to
nature can have self-reported

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.200
psychological benefits, but
there was a dearth of data

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.960
on changes in objective measurements;
so, I was excited to see this paper

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:42.400
on the effects on levels of the
stress hormone cortisol in the saliva

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.160
of those partaking in
“forest bathing,” which just means

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:49.360
visiting a forest and
surrounding yourself by trees.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.840
The level of cortisol in your
saliva is considered an indicator

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.760
of your stress level, and
after walking in a forest,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.680
compared to walking in a city,
or even after just hanging out

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.280
in a forest compared to a city,
people’s salivary cortisol levels

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.920
were significantly lower.
But wait a second,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.400
the same effect was found
before they went to the forest. Huh?

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.920
Forest bathing was associated with
significantly lower salivary cortisol

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.240
both before and after, compared
with visiting an urban area.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.560
Therefore, it appears that just the
thought of going to spend the day

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.440
in the forest relieved stress.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.760
So, when comparing the effects of
forest bathing versus urban visiting,

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.120
the anticipation placebo effect
may play a more important role

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.880
in influencing stress levels
than the actual experience

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.880
of being in the forest.
So, I was ready to dismiss it

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:43.960
as just another nebulous psychological
effect until...I read this.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.240
Studies on the effects of forest
bathing on the immune function

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.440
showed that visiting a forest
can induce a significant increase

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.360
in the number and activity
of natural killer cells,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.760
one of the ways our
body fights off cancer.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.280
That got my attention.
It all started with this study.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.920
Twelve men were taken on a long
weekend to walk in some forests,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.920
and almost all of the subjects
(11 out of 12) showed higher

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.800
natural killer cell activity after
the trip, and not just a little—

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.920
about a 50% increase
compared to before the trip.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.760
Now, just exercise can
affect immune function,

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.960
but they weren’t walking any extra;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.480
they were just walking
in a forest instead.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.760
Yeah, but they also were
taken on a trip somewhere,

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.120
introducing other variables.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.760
So, how about randomizing
them to go on

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.600
some city trip versus
the forest trip.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.000
And if there is some
special forest effect,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.760
how long does the effect last?

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.240
Do you have to like walk
in the forest every day?

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.440
Before jumping into all that,
how about we first see

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.040
if it works in women too.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.320
Same kind of set-up, and same
results: a significant boost

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.120
in natural killer cell activity
walking around in the woods.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:56.160
And this time they went back a
week later to retest them,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.080
and they were still up, though
after a month they came back down.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.920
But hey, once a week should do it,
but it was a multiple-day trip.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.120
Who has time to hang out in
forests all weekend, every weekend?

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.520
How about just a little day trip?
The title gives it all away.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:18.680
Boom! Same thing! The same big jump
measured the day after the trip

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.680
compared to before and with
the same staying power.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.360
Natural killer cell activity
still boosted a week later.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.440
This suggests that if people
visit a suburban forest park

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.400
once a week on a day trip,
they may be able to maintain

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.640
the increased anti-cancer immunity.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.880
OK, but I’m still not convinced.
How can you attribute the benefit

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:44.360
to the forest itself, when all
you have is before and after data?

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.880
To make the case that nature
had anything to do with it

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.440
you’d need a control group
that took the same kind of trip

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.160
but went to somewhere else instead.
And… here we go.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.600
It turns out visiting a
forest, but not a city,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.640
increases human natural
killer activity.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.120
Here’s the forest data
just like before,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.360
but nothing on a trip to
go walking in a city.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:09.440
By the end of the forest trip,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.960
the subjects experienced a boost of 80%
in NK activity after forest bathing,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.600
compared to only a 10% bump
for the city walkers.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:20.280
And both trips were matched for
physical activity, and alcohol,

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.880
and sleep—other things that
can affect immune function.

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:26.880
And so, here we go.
Confirmation of boosted immunity,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.240
but only on the forest trip,
“indicating that forest bathing

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:34.000
does indeed enhance [natural
killer cell] activity.”

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.560
Moreover, they found that
the increased activity lasted

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.960
up to 30 days after the
trip. Check it out.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.760
Still up a week later and
even a bit up a month later.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.880
“This suggests that if people
visit a forest once a month,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.280
they may be able to maintain increased
[natural killer cell] activity.”

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.600
OK, so, now that we know
that it’s a real effect,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.440
the next question is, why?

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.480
What is it about forests
that gives us the boost?

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.280
And (you can imagine Big Pharma
thinking) can you make it into a pill?

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:13.240
We’ll find out next.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.960
Studies on the effects of forest bathing---
a traditional practice in Japan

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.160
of visiting a forest and breathing
its air---have found it can induce

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.320
significant increases in the number
and activity of natural killer cells

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.380
that can last for as long as a month.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.720
And because natural killer cells are one
of the ways your body fights cancer

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.080
by killing off tumor cells, the
findings suggest that forest visits

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.320
may have a preventive effect on
cancer generation and progression.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.000
OK, but how? Why did the
forest environment increase

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:48.240
natural killer cell activity? What is
it about the forest environment?

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.120
One thought is that the boost may
be related to a reduction in stress.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.360
If you measure the amount of adrenaline
flowing through people's systems,

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:59.280
did hanging out in a forest, but not
in a city, drop adrenaline levels down?

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.240
Yes, so that checks out, but drip some
adrenaline on human blood cells

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.880
in a Petri dish, and there does
not appear to be any effect.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.600
The stress hormone, cortisol,
on the other hand,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.320
dramatically suppresses
natural killer cell activity.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.120
So maybe the forest led to less
stress, less cortisol, which released

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.240
the natural killer cells under its
thumb, and you get that big boost?

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:26.280
We know being surrounded by nature can
decrease levels of cortisol in our saliva,

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:31.160
but what about our bloodstream?
A significant drop after a single day

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:35.120
trip to the forest. But a week later
the cortisol was normalizing,

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.320
and the forest effects sometimes
appeared to last an entire month.

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.840
Anything else that could cause a
longer-term immune system change?

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.920
Maybe we've been missing
some of our old friends.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.240
If you sample outdoor air, you can pick
up an abundance of microorganisms

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.280
floating around from the soil or water,
which are absent in indoor air

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:01.440
(which is dominated by organisms that
either live on us or trying to attack us).

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.240
So maybe on a day-to-day basis,
in terms of keeping our immune system

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.120
on ready alert, it might not be sufficient
to encounter only the biased microbes of

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.920
the modern synthetic indoor environment
that lacks some of the old friends,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.160
and probably bears little resemblance
to the microbes we evolved to live with

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.760
over millions of years.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.560
Or maybe it's the plants themselves.
Maybe it's the aroma of the forest.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:30.640
Trees produce aromatic volatile
compounds called phytoncides like pinene,

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.760
which you can breathe into
your lungs in the forest.

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:37.520
But do these compounds actually
get into your bloodstream?

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.120
One hour in the woods and
you get like a 6-fold increase

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:43.560
in circulating pinene levels
circulating throughout your system.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:48.680
OK, but to fully connect all the
dots, the phytoncides like pinene,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.600
these tree essential oils
would have to then induce

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.920
human natural killer cell activity.
And guess what?

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.440
Phytoncides induce human
natural killer cell activity.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.960
If you stick natural killer
cells in a Petri dish

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.520
with some unsuspecting leukemia
cells, your killers can wipe out

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.600
some of the cancer cells. But add
a whiff of cypress, white cedar,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:16.760
eucalyptus, or pine and the
cancer cells don't stand a chance.

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:21.080
A combination of wood aromas
improved the recovery of mice

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.240
put through the wringer, but this
is the study I was looking for.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.560
If we want to know
if the magic ingredient

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.280
is the fragrance of the forest,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.120
then let's see if we can get that same
boost in natural killer cell activity

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:37.480
just vaporizing some essential
oil from one of the trees

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:42.600
into a hotel room overnight.
And it worked! A significant boost

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.440
in natural killer cell activity, though
it just boosted their activity

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:50.280
rather than their number, and being
in the actual forest can do both.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.680
So maybe it's a combination

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:56.080
of the tree fragrance and the lower
cortisol levels working together?

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.360
Ironically, these phytoncide compounds
are part of the tree's own immune system,

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.600
which we may be able to commandeer.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:07.740
The researchers speculate these
compounds may be playing some role

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:11.560
in the fact that more heavily forested
regions in Japan appeared to have

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.960
lower death rates from breast
cancer and prostate cancer.

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.240
Being out in nature has been found
to be an important coping strategy

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.640
among cancer patients. It turns out
this could potentially be helping more

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:29.360
than just with the coping,
thanks to the fragrance of trees.

